    {
      "id": "concept-01-test-isolation",
      "category": "best-practices",
      "prompt": "What are the best practices for test data isolation in Playwright?",
      "answer": "Based on the provided context, the best practices for test data isolation in Playwright include:\n\n1. **Two-layer isolation strategy**:\n   - **Layer 1 — Dynamic API-driven seeding**: Create unique workspaces, users, and contexts for each test suite using REST/GraphQL. Append a cryptographic seed or timestamp to every name to prevent record collisions.\n   - **Layer 2 — Isolated browser context pooling**: Ensure each Playwright worker runs in its own browser context, scoping cookies, localStorage, and IndexedDB to the context to prevent bleed between parallel tests.\n\n2. **Cleanup contract**: Every fixture that creates state should register a teardown, preferably using Playwright's `auto` fixture pattern to ensure cleanup runs even on test failure. Use API DELETE rather than UI flows for teardown speed.\n\n3. **Code example**:\n```ts\ntest.beforeEach(async ({ request }, testInfo) => {\n  const seed = `${testInfo.title.replace(/\\W/g, '-')}-${Date.now()}`;\n  const workspace = await request.post('/api/workspaces', { data: { name: seed } });\n  testInfo.attach('workspace', { body: JSON.stringify(workspace) });\n  return async () => request.delete(`/api/workspaces/${workspace.id}`);\n});\n```\n\n4. **Trade-offs**:\n   - **Static tenant mapping**: Zero setup latency but high parallel race risk, best for read-only smoke tests.\n   - **Dynamic API seed**: Total independence and zero state leaks, with slight setup time increase, best for core regression and CRUD.\n   - **DB snapshots**: Identical state across baselines but complex Docker/infra, best for nightly batch and performance testing.\n\nThese practices help in reducing flakiness and ensuring reliable evidence about release risk in enterprise automation.",
